  "body": "There’s a weird example for the `<strong>` element in the [spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/text-level-semantics.html#the-strong-element):\r\n\r\n> In this example, the heading is really \"Flowers, Bees, and Honey\", but the author has added a light-hearted addition to the heading. The strong element is thus used to mark up the first part to distinguish it from the latter part.\r\n>\r\n>```\r\n><h1><strong>Flowers, Bees, and Honey</strong> and other things I don't understand</h1>\r\n>```\r\n\r\nIt’s weird that `<strong>` is applied to „Flowers, Bees, and Honey“ to **lower** the weight of „and other things I don't understand“. Why this is a problem may be more obvious with some context as in:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<h2><strong>Flowers, Bees, and Honey</strong> and other things I don't understand</h2>\r\n<p>…</p>\r\n<h2>Bears, Salmon and Eggs</h2>\r\n<p>…</p>\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat it’s supposed to mean is that „Flowers, Bees, and Honey“ and „Bears, Salmon and Eggs“ are headings of equal weight. But that’s not what it says and looks like. It seems to say that „and other things I don't understand“ and „Bears, Salmon and Eggs“ are of equal weight, while „Flowers, Bees, and Honey“ has more weight.\r\n\r\nHow would I mark up the desired meaning appropriately? And can we change the example then?",
